# Flutter verify on every change

After **any** edit under `mobile/` (or shared packages consumed by the mobile app), before telling the user the work is done:

1. **Analyze** (must report zero issues — no errors, no warnings, no info):

   ```bash
   cd mobile && ./tool/flutterw analyze --fatal-infos .
   ```

   Prefer `./tool/flutterw` over a global `flutter` binary. For a one-file tweak, analyzing that path (or package) is enough when the change cannot affect other packages.

2. **Test — targeted only** (agent default):

   ```bash
   cd mobile && ./tool/flutterw test test/<relevant>_test.dart
   ```

   Run the test file(s) that cover the changed logic (and any new tests you added). Do **not** run the full suite
   `./tool/flutterw test` as a delivery gate — it is slow in the agent environment; the developer runs it
   periodically.

3. **Iterate** until analyze and the chosen targeted tests are green. Do not hand off with a failing analyze
   or a failing targeted test you were required to run.

## Skip targeted tests when they cannot matter

**Do not** run tests when it is **obvious** that the change cannot affect test outcomes — for example:

- Pure layout swap with no logic, routing, or widget identity change that tests assert (e.g. moving an existing link from left to right on the same row)
- Comments, docs-only wording in Dart comments, or formatting/whitespace-only edits
- Debug/QA copy that no unit/widget test reads

Still run **analyze** on touched Dart. If there is any reasonable doubt, run the **relevant targeted** file(s). Prefer skipping useless test runs after a trivial UI tweak.

This skip does **not** apply to Maestro / `qa:run-*` (separate policy in Maestro skills).

## Full suite — developer-owned; agent reminds on large changes

The **full** suite (`cd mobile && ./tool/flutterw test`) is the developer’s job from time to time — **not** the agent’s default.

When the change is **large** (broad refactor, many packages/files, shared infra such as Drift/schema, relay client, seed plumbing used widely, or several unrelated behaviors in one delivery), **remind the user in the delivery message** to run the full suite locally, e.g.:

> Gros changement — pense à rouler `cd mobile && ./tool/flutterw test` de ton côté.

Do **not** start the full suite yourself unless the user explicitly asks in that turn.

## If an existing test fails

- If the failure is **clearly caused by your change**, fix the product code or update the test as appropriate and re-run the **targeted** test until green.
- If the failure appears **unrelated** to your change, **stop** and tell the user which test failed (name + file + short error). Do **not** change that test unless they explicitly allow it.

## Delivery bar

Only report completion when `flutter analyze --fatal-infos` succeeds with no remaining issues, and either the **targeted** tests you ran succeeded or the **skip rule** above clearly applied (state that briefly when skipping). On large changes, include the full-suite reminder.

## QA APK native SQLite (after clean / APK rebuild)

`analyze` / `test` do **not** prove the Android QA APK can open Drift. After
`flutter clean` or any rebuild that refreshes `mobile/build/native_assets/**`
or `app-dev-debug.apk`, if the user will run Maestro / `qa:run-*`:

1. Extract `lib/x86_64/libsqlite3.so` from the APK (and preferably `arm64-v8a`).
2. Confirm `readelf -d` shows **`NEEDED … libc.so`** (Android).  
   **`libc.so.6`** means a Linux host binary was packaged — seeds will hang on
   Setup with `seed_applied` empty (see
   `no-probability-coding.mdc` founder incident, 2 Aug 2026).
3. Do **not** hand off “re-run the scenario” until that check passes (or say
   explicitly that the APK was not rebuilt / not verified).
4. Do **not** also place `libsqlite3.so` under
   `mobile/android/app/src/main/jniLibs` while Flutter installs the same name
   via `native_assets` — that fails `mergeDevDebugJniLibFolders` with Duplicate
   resources (`mobile/terminal.log` 2026-08-02 18:43). One path only.
